###### 3.8.2.2 Example: Example 10.21, *Romberg Quadrature*, in: Richter et al., 2024, Ch 10.3, p.467

Richter (2024, p.467) reports for the value of the integral...

$I(f) = \int_0^1 exp(1.0 + sin^2(x)) dx \approx 4.7662017991376737307$

... but with *WolframAlpha* we get $I(f)=3.66242$ ! Only when we shift the upper limit to $1.1848$ we get $I(f)=4.76637.$

We get nearly the same results as with *WolframAlpha* with our function $myIntegral(.)$, the functions $romberg, integrate$ and $quadgk$ of packages $Romberg.jl$, $NumericalIntegration.jl$, and $QuadGK.jl$.

