# ╔═╡ 72f96d67-c17e-4e02-8864-5d15db7cde73
md"
---
##### 3.9 Implementing *Antiderivatives*
(cf. Walck, 2023, p.81, p.85f)

$F(x) = C + \int f(x) dx\;\tag{6.3}$

where: $F(x)$ is the antiderivative of $f(x)$ or short:

$DF = f.$

###### [Fundamental Theorem of Calculus](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fundamental_theorem_of_calculus)

$F(b) - F(a) = \int_a^b f(x) dx$

###### Antiderivative $F(x)$

$F(x) = F(a) + \int_a^x f(x') dx'\tag{6.4}$

###### Example: Antiderivative $velocity(t) = F(acceleration(t))$

$v(t) = \int_0^t a(t')dt'\;\tag{6.1}$

where:

$v = \text{velocity}\;; a = \text{acceleration}$
"

# ╔═╡ 78e74973-9779-40a2-9e0b-f01f469b1958
md"
---
###### 3.9.1 Methods of Function Antiderivative $F(x)$

"

# ╔═╡ 12e35f50-b5e3-4a44-a05d-5a61d39c2804
md"
###### 1. *Method* of Function Antiderivative $F(x)$ with *Continuous* Function Parameter $f(x)$

$F(x) = F(a) + \int_a^x f(x') dx' \approx c + \Delta x \sum_{i=1}^x f(x_i)\;;\; x_i \le x$

where:

$c = F(a).$
"

# ╔═╡ 1c5f5df9-53ac-4a87-a695-9874fb1266e2
antiDerivative(   
#   +-----+--------------------------------------------------- functional argument f
#   |     |                   +------------------------------- lower limit a
#   |     |                   |           +------------------- upper limit b = x
#	|     |                   |           |           +------- integration constant c
#   |     |                   |           |           |
#   v     v                   v           v           v                        
	f::Function, Δx::Float64, a::Float64, x::Float64; c::Float64=0.0)::Float64 = 
	myIntegral(f, Δx, a, x, c = c)      

# ╔═╡ daffe586-9327-4289-be04-1e5e3e0d1764
md"
###### 2. Method of Function Antiderivative $F(x)$ with *Vector* of Discretized *Function* Values 

There is a *constraint* between upper integration limit $x = b - a$, $length([...,f_x[i],...]\ and\ \Delta x$:

$x = length([...,f_x[i],...]) \cdot \Delta x = n \Delta x$

and mappings

$(x, \frac{1}{\Delta x}) \mapsto n \left(= length([...,f_xx[i],...])\right)$

$(x, n) \mapsto \frac{x}{n} = \Delta x.$

So the second *method* of function $antiDerivative$ has *two* values:
- the *first* value is the *approximation* of *antiderivative* 

$F(x)\approx c + \Delta x ∑_{i=1}^n f_x[i]$

- the *second* value is the *reconstruction* of the integration bound $x$ dependent on the *length* $n$ of the *vector* of function values $length([...,f_xx[i],...])$ and step length $\Delta x$ 

$length([...,f_xx[i],...]) \cdot \Delta x = n\Delta x \mapsto x$

The *approximation* of the antiderivative is:

$F(x) = F(a) + \int_a^x f(x') dx' \approx c + \Delta x \sum_{i=1}^n [...,f(x)[i],...].$
"

# ╔═╡ 1a70df53-a323-4a25-9a45-f26b61c7ce18
antiDerivative(
#   +-----+--------------------------------------------- vector of function values 
#   |     |
#   v     v
	fs::Vector{Float64}, Δx::Float64; c::Float64=0.0)::Tuple{Float64, Float64} =
	myIntegral(fs, Δx, c = c)[1], myIntegral(fs, Δx,  c = c)[2]   # ==> (F(x), x)

