# ╔═╡ 8f5d4e99-8a6d-42b8-bce2-862c39946ba8
md"
---
###### 3.9.3 Antiderivatives in the *Scotch-Yoke-Oscillator*

###### 3.9.3.1 Dimensionless *Time* $\tau$
We define a dimensionless *time*

$\tau = \omega t$

and normalize 

$amplitude\ r = 1$

and

$angular\ velocity\ \omega = 1.$

This removes all physical units and keeps only the shape of the motion.

---
###### 3.9.3.2 Normalized Kinematic Functions

There is a kind of *hierarchy of derivatives* $x'(τ), x''(τ), x'''(τ)$ in the context of the *Scotch-Yoke-Oscillator*:

- [*jerk*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jerk_(physics)) function
$x'''(τ) = jerk(τ) =  sin(τ)$                
- *acceleration* function
$x''(τ) = acc(τ)  = -cos(τ)$                
- *velocity* function
$x'(τ)  = vel(τ)  = -sin(τ)$                
- *position* function
$x(τ) = pos(τ)  =  cos(τ)$    

When we start from $x'''(t) = jerk(t)$ we can derive $x(t)$ by working *backwards* computing successive *antiderivatives*.
"

# ╔═╡ 9d2911d5-25de-469d-a0cc-73d61af8c0ab
let Δτ      =  1.0E-3                         # should not be smaller than 1.0E-4 !
	τUpLim  =  2.0pi                          # upper integration limit τ
	τs      =  [τ for τ  in 0.0:Δτ:τUpLim]    # τUpLim/Δτ = n integration step points
	jerk(τ) =  sin(τ)                         # x'''(τ) = jerk in Scotch-Yoke-Oscill.
	acc(τ)  = -cos(τ)                         # acceleration
	vel(τ)  = -sin(τ)                         # velocity
	pos(τ)  =  cos(τ)                         # position 
	#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	plot(xlimits=[-0.02, 6.52], ylimits=[-1.22, 1.76], title=L"State\ Function\ and\ Derivatives\ of\ Scotch-Yoke-Oscillator", titlefontsize=12, xlabel=L"τ", ylabel=L"j(τ), a(τ), v(τ), x(τ)")
	#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
	plot!(τs, τ->jerk(τ), lw=1.5, color=:red, label=L"j(τ)")
	plot!(τs, τ->acc(τ),  lw=1.5, color=:green, label=L"a(t)")
	plot!(τs, τ->vel(τ),  lw=1.5, color=:orange, label=L"v(t)")
	plot!(τs, τ->pos(τ),  lw=1.5, color=:blue, label=L"x(t)")
	#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
end # let

# ╔═╡ 699c4629-5f85-4e14-987b-38e9933deada
md"
How to read the figure:
- **Position $x(\tau)$ (blue) and Acceleration $a(\tau)$ (green)** are 180 degrees out of phase: When the *position* is at a maximum, *acceleration* is at a minimum and vice versa.
- **Velocity $v(\tau)$ (orange) and Jerk $j(\tau)$ (red)** are also 180 degrees out of phase: When *velocity* is at a maximum, *jerk* is minimal and vice versa.
- **Maximum Jerk**: The *jerk* reaches its *maximum magnitude* when the slider passes through the *mid-stroke position* $x=0$. At this point *acceleration* crosses zero and changes sign.
- **Zero Jerk**: *Jerk* is zero when *acceleration* is at an *extremum* (maximum or minimum). This means that the acceleration is momentarily not changing.
"

